WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.790
Avengers: Endgame co-director Joe Russo has
a few choice words for Oscar voters.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:08.651
Critics are near-unanimous in their praise
of the film and Robert Downey, Jr.'s performance

00:00:08.651 --> 00:00:13.019
as Tony Stark but if Russo were to have his
way, the Marvel Cinematic Universe's biggest

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.100
star would take home an Academy Award for
the flick, according to The Washington Post.

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:19.760
Heads up some spoilers for Avengers: Endgame
ahead.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.060
During a stop in the nation's capital on a
promotional tour for the film late last week,

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:28.010
Russo didn't hold back about the contributions
his star made not only to Endgame, but to

00:00:28.010 --> 00:00:31.040
the Marvel Cinematic Universe and popular
culture in general.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:31.780
He explained,

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:34.720
"His cumulative body of work from these movies
is staggering.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.940
If you look at the work over just even the
last four [Marvel] films he's done, it's phenomenal…

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.740
He deserves an Oscar perhaps more than anyone
in the last 40 years because of the way that

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.450
he has motivated popular culture."

00:00:44.450 --> 00:00:48.550
There have been a lot of great film performances
in the last 40 years, and Russo's making quite

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:50.520
a bold statement but he does have a point.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:54.860
Downey kicked off the entire MCU with what
was seen at the time as an improbably brilliant

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.810
performance in 2008's Iron Man, and since
then, he has added layers of depth and complexity

00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.280
to a character that could have been more one-note
in the hands of a lesser actor.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:07.580
According to Cinemablend, his casting was
seen by industry analysts as a gamble when

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:09.260
it was announced in 2007.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:11.300
"I am Iron Man"

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.040
In the decade leading up to his first triumphant
turn as Stark, Downey had struggled heavily

00:01:20.050 --> 00:01:25.000
with alcohol abuse, a poor public image, and
a tendency to confuse and alienate those around

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.860
him all issues which had plagued Stark for
years in the pages of Marvel comics.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:33.080
Now, Russo and his co-director brother Anthony
Russo believe Downey's work should be taken

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:35.420
into consideration by the Academy's voters.

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:36.220
Anthony said,

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:41.060
"He has a very involved process a more involved
process than any actor we've ever worked with.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:43.580
He digs deeper, and he works harder than anybody.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:47.360
[...] Downey really just goes the extra mile
for everything it's like everything he's doing

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:48.550
is something to his core."

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:52.990
Joe Russo also made the point that the Academy
seems to have an inherent bias against "popular"

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:56.890
movies in the modern era, compared to past
Oscar Best Picture winners like The Sound

00:01:56.890 --> 00:01:59.920
of Music, The Godfather, and Rocky, to name
just a few.

00:01:59.980 --> 00:02:00.480
He added,

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.280
"We're talking about some of the most popular
films ever made.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.500
It feels like there's now a prejudice against
popular cinema.

00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:08.019
In a way, that's somewhat sad."

00:02:08.019 --> 00:02:12.770
Downey has been nominated for Oscars twice
before: once for the title role in the 1992

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:17.620
biopic Chaplin and again for his controversial
comedic turn in 2008's Tropic Thunder.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:21.980
But he permeated the role of Tony Stark with
his own personality, and with Endgame, he

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:25.860
masterfully brought a close to an arc that
has taken over ten years to complete.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:28.640
"Part of the journey is the end."

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:35.060
It seems almost inconceivable now, but when
Marvel Studios chose to lead off their cinematic

00:02:35.070 --> 00:02:39.370
universe with Iron Man, the character was
so obscure that the choice left industry analysts

00:02:39.370 --> 00:02:40.480
scratching their heads.

00:02:40.590 --> 00:02:44.360
In the lead-up to its release, Marvel even
commissioned short animated films intended

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.420
to raise the character's public profile.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:50.440
Today, the character has become a household
name, one of the most popular in all of mainstream

00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:54.290
cinema and it almost certainly would never
have happened if Downey had not poured his

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:58.060
heart and soul into the character every time
he stepped in front of the cameras for Marvel.

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:03.060
Over the last decade-plus, Downey has delivered
an acting performance unlike any in film history

00:03:03.060 --> 00:03:06.560
and for that, we agree that he deserves his
industry's highest honor.

